# Function Parameters: Prefer Object Params

When defining functions, **use a single destructured object parameter** instead of multiple positional parameters when any of the following apply. **Inline the params type** in the function signature (don't extract a separate type unless it's reused elsewhere):

- The function has **more than three parameters**
- **Parameter order is confusing** (e.g. callers might mix up similar types)
- The **number of parameters is variable** or many are optional
- It's **not obvious** what each argument is from the function name alone

Use positional parameters only when the call site is very clear (e.g. `log(message)` or `parseInt(str, radix)`).

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - four positional params, order is easy to mix up
function sendDiscordInfo(
  webhookUrl: string,
  message: string,
  title?: string,
  color?: number
): Promise<void> {}

// ✅ GOOD - single object param with inline type, callers can specify in any order
function sendDiscordInfo({ 
  webhookUrl, 
  message, 
  title, 
  color 
}: {
  webhookUrl: string;
  message: string;
  title?: string;
  color?: number;
}): Promise<void> {
  // ...
}

// Call site - clear and order-independent
await sendDiscordInfo({
  webhookUrl: env.DISCORD_LOGGING_WEBHOOK_URL,
  message: "Done",
  title: "Success",
  color: 0x10b981,
});
```

```typescript
// ✅ OK - few params, obvious from name
function formatEventDate(dateString: string): string {}
function getReportableEvents(calendarId: string, asOfDate?: Date): Promise<LumaEvent[]> {}
```
